CLAUDE.md
RULES.md
task.md
PLAN.md
PLAN-*.md
.claude/

# A local set of captures for the offline proofs to run against. These are NOT
# this project's to publish: a capture is a recording made by whoever trained
# it, under their own terms, and the code's MIT grant does not reach it. Neither
# product ships any and neither resolves a bank by a tracked path — the four
# banks are whatever the user loaded — so nothing here depends on this directory
# existing. The proofs find it through --captures or $RATIONS_TEST_CAPTURES.
#
# Keep it that way: a capture committed here is a capture republished to
# everyone who clones, and deleting it later does not take it back out of the
# history.
captures/

# The ASIO SDK, which must NEVER be committed here, in whole or in part.
#
# This is a licence condition and not tidiness. Steinberg's agreement forbids the licensee to
# "sell, license, give-away and/or distribute the SDK or parts of it for the use as software
# developer kit in any way, for example, integrated in any framework application, on any medium,
# including the Internet" — so a vendored copy, a checked-in header, or a stray asio.h anywhere in
# the working tree is a breach, and unlike most mistakes in this tree, pushing one cannot be taken
# back by a later deletion. (The reference implementations get this wrong: RtAudio vendors an ASIO
# snapshot in-repository and iPlug2 inherits it. It is not a precedent to follow.)
#
# The SDK reaches the Windows build through the rack's scripts/build-win-deps.sh and the untracked
# dependency sysroot, and through nothing else. These names are guarded so a stray copy cannot be
# staged by accident; the phase gate sweeps for the same names, because the ignore rule stops a
# copy arriving and the sweep catches one that arrived another way.
#
# AT THE ROOT AND UNANCHORED, deliberately. Only one product builds for ASIO today, but a stray
# copy can land anywhere in a tree this size, and a rule that only guarded products/rack/ would
# not have been watching the place the accident happened.
#
# Our own backend is asiobackend.{h,cpp}, which none of these patterns matches.
asio.h
asio.cpp
asiosys.h
iasiodrv.h
asiodrvr.h
asiodrvr.cpp
asiodrivers.h
asiodrivers.cpp
asiolist.h
asiolist.cpp
ASIOConvertSamples.h
ASIOConvertSamples.cpp

